{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792891753,"nonce":"F4y29ume0G1RUClLfE89uVdcb3Y=","message":"Correction: Ash's layout scan (e53ebf70) is on by default only on 32-bit targets (precise_objects(): ASH_GC_PRECISE unset means cfg!(target_pointer_width = \"32\")), and caribou already has 2963f365's pointer-free class tables (alloc_immortal_noptr, RT_VERSION 8). On 64-bit macOS both heaps scan class instances word by word, so neither commit explains the gap.\n\nChimpMonk's ASH_GC_OCCUPANCY=1 at 'every panel open' on b9c2753 (second major): ash retained=4172 blocks (130.4 MB), marked_lines=290313 (35.4 MB, 27.2%); caribou retained=6238 blocks (194.9 MB), marked_lines=384261 (46.9 MB, 24.1%). About 70% of the block gap is extra marked data (1.32x lines), about 30% packing (61.6 against 69.6 marked lines per block); the extra blocks are the sparse ones (5-16: 745 against 362, 17-64: 3421 against 2075).\n\nSomething caribou adds reaches more: candidates are roots only caribou has (its root ranges, span readers, handles, bridge cells, more mutator stacks). ASH_GC_DEBUG_ROOTS now reports, per root group, the KB it keeps alive that no earlier group had, in Ash's format, so the two runs can be compared group by group.","files":null}]}